nstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "database-foundations-m1-l1": {"trace": "CREATE TABLE defines the structure but adds no books. INSERT adds three rows. SELECT asks for two columns and ORDER BY id fixes the display order. The rows are Python/100, Databases/150, and Web/100. A result table is a view of stored data, not a second saved copy.", "practice": "Read only the title column and keep the rows ordered by id.", "review": "Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."}, "database-foundations-m2-l1": {"trace": "The update changes the title of row 1 only. Its id stays 1, so references remain meaningful. Without WHERE, every title would change. In a fresh practice copy, try inserting another row with id 1 and expect a key constraint failure rather than two ambiguous identities.", "practice": "Rename book 2 without changing either its id or the other books.", "review": "Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}, "database-foundations-m3-l1": {"trace": "Foreign key enforcement is enabled before writing. Loan 1 refers to book 2. The join follows that reference and returns Databases. An attempted book_id of 99 fails because no such parent row exists. Deleting a referenced book also requires an explicit relationship policy.", "practice": "Add a second loan for book 1 and list both referenced titles.", "review": "Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}, "database-foundations-m4-l1": {"trace": "Both books reference category 1, so one category name is stored. The join displays Computing twice because two books use it; repeated output is not duplicated storage. This minimal example omits the foreign key deliberately: add it before relying on referential integrity.", "practice": "Rename the shared category once and explain why both result rows change.", "review": "Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}, "database-foundations-m5-l1": {"trace": "The valid row returns Amina and 20. Try an empty name or age -1 in a disposable copy: the corresponding CHECK rejects it. Constraints protect this table, but deciding whether an age is plausible for a specific enrollment still requires a clear application policy.", "practice": "Add a check that rejects a blank book title and test a spaces-only title.", "review": "Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}, "database-foundations-m6-l1": {"trace": "The transaction temporarily changes book 1 to zero. ROLLBACK cancels that change, so the final SELECT returns 100. Replacing ROLLBACK with COMMIT would keep zero. Test both on fresh copies; a successful rollback does not prove that a separate backup can be restored.", "practice": "Compare committed and rolled-back price changes and record both final values.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "database-foundations-m7-l1": {"trace": "This database foundations module teaches you to compare replication, partitioning, consistency, availability, and conflict resolution using explicit failure scenarios. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.", "practice": "Model a partition and recovery scenario, sta