re two price groups. Price 100 has two rows and price 150 has one. COUNT(*) counts rows, not the numeric sum of prices. SUM(price) across the original table is 350. An empty input makes SUM return NULL, so choose an explicit empty-total policy.", "practice": "Calculate total price and explain the difference from the book count.", "review": "Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}, "sql-m4-l1": {"trace": "Python matches two loans; the other books match none. COUNT(l.id) ignores the NULL placeholder from unmatched rows, producing 2, 0, 0. COUNT(*) would count each retained left row and incorrectly suggest one loan for an unloaned book. Choose the counted expression deliberately.", "practice": "Return only books with no loans using the left-join result.", "review": "Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}, "sql-m5-l1": {"trace": "The added column has no supplied values, so all notes are NULL. IS NULL keeps all three rows and COALESCE displays No note. The underlying notes remain NULL. A later empty string would not match IS NULL, demonstrating why empty and unknown need separate rules.", "practice": "Compare a NULL note and an empty note without treating them as equal.", "review": "Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}, "sql-m6-l1": {"trace": "Only id 1 becomes 120; ids 2 and 3 remain 150 and 100. The literal is fixed lesson data. For a real user value use a driver placeholder such as ? with a separate bound value; placeholders are supplied by the calling API, not quoted inside the command.", "practice": "Preview book 3, change its price in a transaction, and verify other rows.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "sql-m7-l1": {