d one improvement you would make after feedback.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "sql-m16-l1": {"trace": "This SQL module teaches you to plan incremental delivery, version data safely, document offline setup, review risks, and maintain the product after its first release. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.", "practice": "Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "sqlite-m1-l1": {"trace": "The fresh fixture contains three books, so the count is 3. Reopening a memory connection would start empty, while reopening a saved file should retain committed rows. Test those lifetimes separately; seeing data in one open connection is not evidence of restart persistence.", "practice": "Compare a temporary database and a file database after closing their connections.", "review": "Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."}, "sqlite-m2-l1": {"trace": "The inserted prices have integer storage type. That observation covers these rows only. To require integer storage, add a CHECK using typeof where appropriate or a suitable strict schema, and test hostile inputs. Do not conclude that a text declaration alone validates every future value.", "practice": "Require integer nonnegative prices and test text and fractional values.", "review": "Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}, "sqlite-m3-l1": {"trace": "The immediate transaction requests the write lock before changing data. Book 1 becomes 90 because it meets the condition. A competing writer must wait or receive a busy error according to connection policy. Test a retry without duplicating the business operation.", "practice": "Test an update whose condition matches no rows and report that outcome accurately.", "review": "Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}, "sqlite-m4-l1": {"trace": "The plan describes how SQLite intends to find matching prices; it is not the list of books. Exact wording can vary by SQLite version and statistics. Compare with the plan before the index and measure a realistic fixture. The result rows should remain the same with or without the index.", "practice": "Compare query plans before and after a price index without changing query results.", "review": "Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}, "sqlite-m5-l1": {"trace": "The new active column gives each of the three old rows the default 1. Existing titles and prices remain. Running the same ALTER blindly a second time fails because the column exists. Track schema version and only apply the relevant step, then test reopening the migrated file.", "practice": "Migrate an old fixture and verify all titles, prices, and defaults after reopening.", "review": "Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}, "sqlite-m6-l1": {"trace": "integrity_check should report ok for this fixture; the business check reports 3 rows and total 350. Structural integrity alone does not prove that the right records were backed up. Record both counts and domain totals and compare them after restoring a separate copy.", "practice": "Restore a training backup and compare its row count and total with the source.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "sqlite-m7-l1": {"trace": "This SQLite module teaches you to inspect query plans, design selective indexes, use FTS carefully, and measure write/read tradeoffs. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.", "practice": "Optimize a measured search workload with indexes or FTS and retain before-and-after plans and timing evidence. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "sqlite-m8-l1": {"trace": "This SQLite module teaches you to configure concurrent readers, execute atomic migrations, verify integrity, and test online backup and corruption recovery. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line 