requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "postgresql-m1-l1": {"trace": "The first query identifies the actual connection, so its values depend on your setup. The temporary table belongs to this session. Inserting one book and selecting its title returns Python. Ending the session removes the temporary table; use a deliberate persistent schema for a real application.", "practice": "Identify the connected database and create a disposable one-row table.", "review": "Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."}, "postgresql-m2-l1": {"trace": "The server supplies the identity and returns the inserted row. In this fresh table the first id is 1 and amount is 20.50. Numeric scale preserves the specified decimal precision; a UI string still needs validation. Try a negative amount and expect a constraint error.", "practice": "Insert a valid expense and demonstrate rejection of a negative one.", "review": "Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}, "postgresql-m3-l1": {"trace": "Three rows remain visible. The running totals are 10, 30, and 35 because each frame begins at the first row and ends at the current row. A plain SUM without a window would give a single total. The explicit id order makes this example deterministic.", "practice": "Add a fourth amount and predict its running total before execution.", "review": "Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}, "postgresql-m4-l1": {"trace": "The transaction locks the selected row, reads 5, reduces it to 3, and commits. This one-session example illustrates the sequence; it does not prove concurrent behavior. Use a shared disposable table and two sessions for a real lock test, since temporary tables are session-local.", "practice": "Demonstrate lock waiting with two sessions on a disposable shared table.", "review": "Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}, "postgresql-m5-l1": {"trace": "The fixture has 1000 rows and an index on price. ANALYZE collects statistics; EXPLAIN then reports the chosen plan. The exact plan depends on the server and statistics, so do not grade by a copied plan string. Verify the returned records separately from performance.", "practice": "Compare a selective query with and without an index on a training fixture.", "review": "Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}, "postgresql-m6-l1": {"trace": "pg_dump writes a custom-format archive. pg_restore reads it into a separately prepared target. These commands require permissions and connection settings and do not create a complete access policy. Compare restored row counts, constraints, and representative reports; archive creation alone is not a successful recovery test.", "practice": "Restore a fictional database to a separate target and test a restricted reader role.", "review": "Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}, "postgresql-m7-l1": {"trace": "This PostgreSQL module teaches you to interpret EXPLAIN ANALYZE, choose index families, maintain statistics, and partition only for measured needs. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain a